  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Protein Powder",
    tags: [
      "protein powder packets",
      "single serve protein powder packets",
      "protein powder stick packs",
      "travel protein powder",
      "protein powder TSA",
      "protein powder for travel"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Buyer's Guide"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Best Protein Powder Packets</Fragment>
      Single-serve protein packets trade a higher price per serving for zero scooping, zero mess and an easy trip through airport security. Here are the packs worth carrying, and when a tub and a small container make more sense.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#quick-compare">Quick Comparison</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per Serving vs Tubs</a>
      <a href="#tsa">Protein Powder and TSA</a>
      <a href="#who-for">When Packets Make Sense</a>
      <a href="#types">Whey, Clear or Plant Packets</a>
      <a href="#choosing">What to Look For</a>
      <a href="#diy">The DIY Alternative</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Short answer: Clean Simple Eats Whey Isolate Stick Packs are the best protein powder packets for most people, with about 20 g of grass-fed whey isolate per stick and flavors buyers rate highly. Ascent's native whey packets are the pick if you want 25 g and a sport certification, Orgain's travel packs are the budget and dairy-free choice, and SEEQ or Oath are the packets to buy if you prefer a light, juice-style clear whey. Two things to know before you stock up. Packets almost always cost noticeably more per serving than the same powder in a tub, so they are a convenience buy, not a daily one. And at US airport checkpoints, TSA allows protein powder in carry-ons; it is containers over 12 oz that go in a separate bin and may get extra screening, which a handful of small packets avoids.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="quick-compare" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Protein Powder Packets Compared at a Glance
          </h2>

          <p>
            Here are the seven single-serve products on our list. Protein, calories and sugar are per the label or listing at the time of writing and vary by flavor. Because every packet costs more than a tub, price tier here compares packets with each other per serving: budget is among the cheapest packets, premium among the most expensive.
          </p>

