          <p>
            The quick read: creamy whey packets suit milk-based shakes and are the most versatile. Clear whey packets mix into cold water like a sports drink, which is handy when you have no milk or fridge. Plant packets cover dairy-free travelers. If you want the most protein per stick, Ascent and Happy Viking lead; if you want the cheapest stick, Orgain does.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Best Protein Powder Packets
          </h2>

          <p>
            We ranked these on the label and on availability as single-serve packs on Amazon, not on hands-on testing: protein per packet, calories, protein type, flavor range, third-party testing and price tier per serving among packets at the time of writing. Our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test</a> page explains the method. Nutrition figures are per the label or brand listing and vary by flavor, and comments on taste and mixing reflect buyer feedback. Pack counts and listings change often, so check the box size and per-serving price on the listing before you buy.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Protein Powder Packets vs Tubs: What Convenience Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Single-serve packets almost always cost more per serving than the same brand's tub, and sometimes much more. You are paying for individual foil packaging, smaller boxes and the convenience of pre-measured doses. Sampler and variety packs, which bundle several flavors, tend to sit at the top of the range per gram of protein, because they are priced as a trial rather than a staple.
          </p>

          <p>
            The way to judge value is cost per serving, or better, cost per 25 g of protein. As an illustration with round numbers rather than current prices: if a tub works out to 1.25 dollars per serving and a ten-packet box of the same powder costs 25 dollars, each packet costs 2.50 dollars, double the tub. If the packets also contain less protein per serving than a full scoop, the real premium is higher still. Our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">best cheap protein powder</a> guide uses the same per-gram method to find the best value tubs.
          </p>

          <p>
            That premium is easy to justify for a week of travel, a gym bag that sees a few shakes a week or a box of samples to find a flavor before buying a tub. It is hard to justify as your everyday protein. If you drink a shake most days, buy the tub for home and use packets only where the convenience genuinely matters.
          </p>

          <p>
            Packets have one hidden saving: they make flavor mistakes cheap. A 5 lb tub of a flavor you dislike is the most expensive protein you will ever buy. Several of the picks on this list, including the SEEQ, Oath and Happy Viking packs, are explicitly sold as a way to try flavors, and used that way they can save you money.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Packets for the road, tubs for the kitchen.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Compare cost per 25 g of protein, not cost per box. Use packets where convenience matters, travel, work, the gym bag, and buy the tub for daily shakes at home.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="tsa" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Can You Bring Protein Powder Through TSA?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes. TSA's own guidance lists protein and energy powders as allowed in both carry-on and checked bags. The catch is size. Per TSA, powder-like substances over 12 oz, or 350 mL, need to go in a separate bin for X-ray screening, may get extra screening, and their containers may be opened. TSA encourages travelers to put non-essential powders over 12 oz in checked bags, and as with every item, the officer at the checkpoint makes the final call.
          </p>

          <p>
            Single-serve packets sidestep most of that. A typical packet holds around 30 to 45 g of powder, so a handful of them is far below the 12 oz threshold, and sealed, labelled commercial packets are easy for an officer to identify. A tub of protein in your carry-on is allowed, but it is more likely to slow you down at the checkpoint.
          </p>

          <p>
            International trips add another layer. Since 2018, TSA has applied extra screening to powders over 12 oz in carry-ons on flights to the US from international last points of departure, and powders that cannot be cleared there are not allowed in the cabin and are thrown away. TSA has said this does not change what is allowed at US airport checkpoints. Other countries set their own rules, so check the airport and airline for the country you are departing from.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mixed shakes are a different matter. Once you add water or milk, a shake counts as a liquid, so the 3.4 oz liquids limit applies at the checkpoint. Carry the powder dry, in its packet or an empty shaker, and add liquid after security. Rules can change, so check tsa.gov before you fly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-for" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. When Protein Powder Packets Make Sense
          </h2>

          <p>
            Travel is the obvious case. A few packets in a carry-on or suitcase take up almost no space, cannot spill all over your clothes the way a loose bag of powder can, and are easy to explain at security. For hotel stays, a packet, a shaker bottle and a carton of milk or a bottle of water from a shop is a reliable breakfast or post-workout shake.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gym bags and offices are the next case. A stick pack in a shaker bottle means you can make a shake straight after training without carrying a scoop or a tub, and packets keep better than powder poured into a shaker hours earlier. A box in a desk drawer covers the afternoon slump without tempting you to the vending machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Packets are also the best way to try a new brand or flavor before committing to a full tub, and the easiest way to control portions if you tend to heap the scoop. And they help in shared households or dorms where a big tub would be in the way.
          </p>

          <p>
            They make less sense as your only protein at home, where a tub is cheaper and produces less packaging waste, and for anyone who needs more than one scoop per shake, since that doubles the already higher cost. If you are new to protein powder generally, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-beginners/">best protein powder for beginners</a> guide covers what to buy for everyday use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Whey, Clear Whey or Plant: Which Packet Type to Pick
          </h2>

          <p>
            Creamy whey packets, such as Clean Simple Eats and Ascent, taste like a classic protein shake and are best mixed with milk, though they work in water too. They are the most versatile for breakfast shakes, oats and smoothies, and whey isolate versions are low in lactose. If you are choosing a whey to travel with, our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> guide covers the tubs these packets are based on.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clear whey packets, such as SEEQ and Oath, mix into cold water into a light, juice-like drink. They are ideal when you have no milk or fridge, for example on a flight, a hike or in a hotel room, and they feel more refreshing after a hot workout. The trade-off is that they need cold water to taste their best and do not work in coffee or oats. Our <a href="/best-clear-protein-powder/">best clear protein powder</a> guide covers the category in depth.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plant packets, such as Orgain and Happy Viking, suit dairy-free and vegan travelers. They tend to be thicker and earthier, and they mix better in a shaker with a mixing ball or in plant milk than in plain water. Plant proteins also tend to test higher for heavy metals than dairy proteins on average, which matters less for occasional travel use than for daily use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Collagen blends such as FlavCity's packets count some collagen toward the protein total. Collagen is fine for convenience, but it is a weaker protein for building muscle than whey, so do not compare its protein total one-for-one with a pure whey packet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What to Look For in Protein Powder Packets
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with protein per packet. Single-serve packets range from around 20 g to 30 g per stick, and that difference matters if a packet is your whole post-workout shake. Around 20 to 25 g of a high-quality protein covers most people's per-meal needs; larger people and older adults may benefit from more, which our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein do I need</a> guide explains.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, check the box count and the per-serving price. Boxes range from four packets to around twenty, and very small sampler boxes cost the most per serving. If you travel often, a larger box of a flavor you already know you like is better value than repeated sampler packs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then consider the format. Narrow stick packs pour straight into a shaker bottle without spilling, while wider pouches can be messier on the move. Look for packets that tear cleanly and are easy to pour from a moving bus or a tight gym locker. Buyer feedback is the best guide here, since brands rarely describe it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, look for third-party testing if you compete in a tested sport. Informed Sport, Informed Choice and NSF Certified for Sport cover banned substances, and certification can differ between a brand's tub and its packets or between flavors, so check the certifier's database for the exact product.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="diy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. The DIY Alternative: Portion Your Own Tub
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you already have a tub you like, portioning it yourself is the cheapest way to travel with protein. A stackable powder container or a shaker bottle with a screw-on powder compartment holds several pre-measured scoops, and small reusable containers or zip bags work too. You pay tub prices, avoid flavor risk and create less packaging waste than single-use packets.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are trade-offs. Unlabelled white powder in a zip bag is more likely to draw attention at airport security than sealed commercial packets, and over 12 oz it needs a separate bin. Keeping the powder in its original labelled packaging, or carrying only small amounts, makes screening smoother. Homemade portions also take a few minutes to prepare and are easier to spill.
          </p>

          <p>
            A practical mix for frequent travelers is a tub at home, a box of packets in the suitcase or gym bag, and a powder container for longer trips where packets would add up. That gives you low cost at home and convenience where it counts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you carry, protein powder is a supplement, not a substitute for meals. If you are pregnant, have kidney disease or take medication, check with your doctor or a registered dietitian before relying on shakes, especially while traveling and eating irregularly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using packets as your everyday protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Single-serve packets cost noticeably more per serving than the same powder in a tub. If you drink a shake most days, buy the tub for home and keep packets for travel, work and the gym bag, where the convenience actually earns its price.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing price per box</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Boxes range from four packets to around twenty, and protein per packet varies from about 20 g to 30 g. Compare cost per 25 g of protein, not the sticker price, or a small sampler box can look cheaper than it really is.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Packing a mixed shake in your carry-on</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Once you add liquid, a shake counts toward the 3.4 oz liquids limit at airport security. Carry the powder dry in its packet or an empty shaker and add water or milk after you clear the checkpoint.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Carrying loose powder in unlabelled bags</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Unlabelled powder is more likely to draw extra screening, and over 12 oz it needs a separate bin. Sealed, labelled commercial packets or small labelled containers make the checkpoint quicker. Check tsa.gov before you fly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a big box of an untried flavor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The point of packets is low-risk sampling. Start with a variety or sample pack to find a flavor you like, then buy a larger box or a tub. A big box of a flavor you dislike wastes the convenience premium.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What are the best protein powder packets?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Clean Simple Eats Whey Isolate Stick Packs are our top pick for most people, with about 20 g of grass-fed whey isolate per stick. Ascent's native whey packets offer 25 g and Informed Sport certification on some versions, Orgain's travel packs are the budget dairy-free option, and SEEQ and Oath make the best clear whey packets.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can you bring protein powder on a plane?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. TSA allows protein powder in carry-on and checked bags. Powders over 12 oz, or 350 mL, must go in a separate bin for X-ray, may get extra screening and may be opened, and TSA suggests checking non-essential powders over that size. A few single-serve packets stay well under the threshold. Check tsa.gov before you fly.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Are protein powder packets worth it?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For travel, the gym bag, the office or trying flavors, yes. As your daily protein, usually not, because packets cost noticeably more per serving than the same powder in a tub. A good approach is a tub at home and a box of packets for the times when convenience matters.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in a protein packet?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Most single-serve protein packets contain about 20 to 30 g of protein. On our list, Clean Simple Eats and Oath are around 20 g, SEEQ 22 g, Orgain 21 g, Ascent 25 g and Happy Viking 30 g, per labels and listings. Check the panel, since figures vary by flavor.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Do protein packets expire?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Like tubs, packets carry a best-by date, often a year or more from manufacture. Sealed foil packets protect the powder from moisture and air well, but heat can degrade flavor, so avoid leaving them in a hot car for long periods. Check the date on the box before stocking up.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can I mix protein packets with just water?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Clear whey packets such as SEEQ and Oath are designed for cold water. Creamy whey packets also mix in water but taste richer with milk. Plant packets are thicker and mix best in a shaker with a mixing ball. Use about 10 to 12 oz of liquid per packet as a starting point.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Are single-serve protein packets bad for the environment?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  They create more packaging waste per serving than a tub, and most foil packets are not curbside recyclable. If that matters to you, use packets only for travel and buy tubs or large bags for home, or portion your own powder into a reusable container for trips.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
